/*! @brief A plain-object dictionary with arbitrary values, keyed by name

 ValueMap is the typed form of dictionaries whose values have no single scalar
 type: the module table of `SandBox`, the connection maps of
 `HttpClient#freeSockets`/`HttpClient#sockets`, the dictionaries returned by
 `os.networkInterfaces` and `net.info`, and the dictionary inputs of
 `HttpCollection#append`, `HttpCollection#set`, `querystring.stringify` and the
 `URLSearchParams`/`FormData` object constructors. It is a regular JavaScript
 object dictionary (not an ES `Map`); the property names are module, field or
 interface names. `util.pick` and `util.omit` stay plain objects because their
 result can carry symbol keys, which a string-keyed map cannot represent.

 ValueMap keeps the value as `any`; it is a migration aid for tables that mix
 module objects, functions, strings, arrays and nested records. A dictionary
 with a known value type should use `StringMap`, `HeaderMap`, `EventMap` or a
 dedicated named map instead.

 Example 1 — pick and omit fields:
 ```JavaScript
 const util = require('util');

 const picked = util.pick({ a: 1, b: 2 }, 'a');
 console.log(picked.a); // 1
 ```

 Example 2 — add modules to a sandbox:
 ```JavaScript
 const vm = require('vm');

 const box = new vm.SandBox({ base: 40 });
 console.log(typeof box.modules.base); // number
 ```
*/
map ValueMap: Value;
